---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import GuideLayout from "../../components/GuideLayout.astro";
import GuideRaceDemo from "../../components/GuideRaceDemo.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";
import { excerpt } from "../../lib/excerpt";
import convergenceSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/test/convergence_test.gleam?raw";

const addRace = excerpt(
  convergenceSource,
  "pub fn concurrent_adds_keep_both_notes_test(",
);

const voteRace = excerpt(
  convergenceSource,
  "pub fn concurrent_plus_plus_minus_votes_settle_at_plus_one_test(",
);
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="race"
  description="Step one of the watershed build guide: run the two concurrency races the tutorial retro board has to survive — a tie between two note adds, and three votes cast at once on one note — then read the checked-in tests that pin both outcomes."
>
  <GuideRaceDemo slot="wide" />

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Start from the thing that breaks</h2>
    <p>
      Most build guides start with a connection string. This one starts with the
      two moments where a retro board stops being a list and starts being a
      distributed system — because every sheet after this one exists to survive
      one of them.
    </p>
    <p>
      The rig above runs two real <code>watershed</code> documents against the
      in-memory <strong>sluice</strong>. Nothing is mocked: the same OR-map
      operations you’ll write in step 03 and step 04 go through the same client
      runtime, with delivery held back so you can watch it happen.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Race one: the tie nobody wins</h2>
    <p>
      Two people type into <em>Went well</em> in the same second and hit Add.
      Both notes are new, neither knows about the other, and there is no
      sensible answer to “which one did they mean?” — because they meant both.
    </p>
    <p>
      Store the board as a plain map keyed by column and you get one note. Not
      because the map is broken: a map with one key per column just <em>is</em>
      a single slot, and last-write-wins is doing exactly what it says. The fix
      is the key, not the merge. Give every note its own id in an
      <strong>OR-map</strong>, and the two adds are not even in conflict.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Race two: the vote you can’t find afterwards</h2>
    <p>
      Now one standing note, three votes in flight: Client A upvotes, Client B
      upvotes, Client B changes their mind and downvotes. Three operations, and
      only one honest total.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read the count, add one, write the count back, and you have written the
      classic distributed-systems bug: two clients read <code>0</code>, both
      write <code>1</code>, and one vote is gone with nothing to show for it.
      A <strong>tally</strong> ships the <code>+1</code> instead of the
      <code>1</code>. Addition commutes, so the order the sequencer picks stops
      mattering — the arithmetic lands the same either way. The demo settles at
      <code>+1</code> whichever way you run it.
    </p>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Want it staged even more sharply?</strong> The{" "}
        <a href="/counter-bug">counter bug</a> page runs the losing version
        first, on purpose, then fixes it. It’s the same argument with the
        failure left in.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Both races are already checked in</h2>
    <p>
      A demo you can’t re-run is a claim, not evidence. Both races above are
      tests in the tutorial project, and they run under <code>gleam test</code>
      in about a second with no server anywhere. Step 06 is where you write
      them; here they’re just proof that the rest of the procedure has to hold
      up.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">test/convergence_test.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={addRace} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
      <figcaption class="annot">retro_tutorial_lustre · add race</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={voteRace} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
      <figcaption class="annot">retro_tutorial_lustre · vote race</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Both start with the same <code>room</code> helper: one sluice, two
      connected documents, a title, a register OR-map, a tally OR-map. Step 06
      unpacks it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>What you’re about to build</h2>
    <p>
      One small application, <strong>retro_tutorial_lustre</strong>, carried
      through all six sheets. Three fixed columns, sticky notes with a vote
      count, and a roster showing who is reading which note. Every step adds one
      capability to the same source tree, and every step ends somewhere you can
      run.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code
        code={`document · retro-tutorial
├── title           String field           the board name, "Sprint retro"
├── notes           OR-map · RegisterMode  note id → the whole note as JSON
├── votes           OR-map · TallyMode     note id → a signed total
└── ↯ focused_note  presence               who is reading which note`}
        lang="text"
        theme={watershedCodeTheme}
      />
    </figure>
    <p>
      It stays deliberately small. There are no sequences, no drag and drop, no
      cross-column moves, no edit or delete, and no vote budgets. When you want
      those, the <a href="/examples#retro_board_lustre">advanced retro board</a>
      {" "}is the same idea with five channels and the harder interactions.
    </p>
    <p>
      You’ll need the <a href="https://gleam.run/getting-started/">Gleam
      toolchain</a> and, from step 02 on, a{" "}
      <a href="https://floodgate.tylerbutler.com">floodgate</a> server. The last
      sheet needs neither a server nor a browser.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">You can now</span>
    <p>
      Name the two concurrency claims this project has to keep — concurrent adds
      both survive, concurrent votes sum — and point at the checked-in test that
      proves each one.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
